list.item:
 Care must be exercised to prevent perivascular                                     infiltration since this can cause inflammation, necrosis and                                     sloughing of tissue. Venospasm and intravenous thrombosis, which                                     may occur during infusion, can be minimized by insuring that the                                     injection needle is well within the largest available vein and                                     that solutions are slowly infused. Intravenous catheters are                                     recommended. If perivascular infiltration occurs, institute                                     appropriate countermeasures (see 
   ADVERSE REACTIONS
).

text.paragraph:
Parenteral drug products should be inspected visually for                             particulate matter and discoloration prior to administration, whenever                             solution and container permit (see 
                        CONTRAINDICATIONS
                     ).
